+    <section class="journal">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to your morning ritual</p>
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div id="journal-story" class="journal-story">
+        <p>A good cup begins long before we switch on the roaster. It starts with the soil, the season, and the people who tend each coffee tree. At The Daily Grind, our sourcing process follows that journey back to its roots. We look for coffees with a clear sense of place and work with producers, cooperatives, and sourcing partners who can tell us how a lot was grown, harvested, and processed. Knowing the story behind the bean helps us make thoughtful decisions about what belongs in your cup.</p>
+        <p>Our first conversations are about more than flavor. We ask about harvest conditions, how coffee cherries are picked, and how growers manage the land that supports them. Shade trees, healthy soil, and careful water use all matter to the long-term future of coffee. We also ask our sourcing partners how prices are agreed and how producers are paid. Our aim is to build relationships that value the work behind a harvest and give us reasons to return season after season, rather than simply chasing the lowest price.</p>
+        <p>Then comes the tasting table. We roast small samples and cup them side by side, paying attention to sweetness, acidity, body, and balance. A washed coffee might offer a bright, clean finish, while a naturally processed lot may bring a rounder, fruit-forward character. We taste each sample more than once and consider how it will brew in the café, whether as a comforting espresso or a slower weekend pour-over. We choose coffees for their quality and character, allowing our selection to change with the harvest instead of expecting every origin to taste the same all year.</p>
+        <p>Once a coffee is selected, we keep track of its origin and processing details as it travels to our roastery. On arrival, we check the green beans and compare a fresh sample with the coffee we originally approved. Storage matters here too: keeping beans cool, dry, and protected helps preserve the qualities that caught our attention in the first place. If a lot arrives tasting different, we revisit our plans before it reaches the menu.</p>
+        <p>Roasting is the final part of that conversation. We develop a profile for each coffee in small batches, adjusting time and heat to bring out its natural sweetness without losing its individual character. We taste the results, refine the profile, and continue checking batches as the coffee ages. What reaches your cup is the result of many small decisions, from choosing a sourcing partner to dialing in a brew. Our journal is a way to share that care, and to keep the people and places behind your daily coffee part of the story.</p>
